    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Malindi was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> town of coral houses and patrician families whose elders, the wazee, elected a chief, the mwenye mui; the Portuguese mistook these men for kings.</li><li>In 1414 the fleet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-he">Zheng He</a> visited the town and Malindi's ruler sent an envoy with a giraffe to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>; in 1498 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a> was warmly received, after his hostile reception in Mombasa, and raised the pillar that still stands.</li><li>Malindi used the Portuguese against its rivals: it supported their conquest of Kilwa and Mombasa in 1505, held their factory from 1502 to 1593, and in 1593 its Sheikh took possession of Mombasa itself.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a> came to Malindi in 1498 to sign a trade agreement and to hire a guide for the voyage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>; the padrão he erected, the Vasco da Gama Pillar, was in 1823 the only vestige of 'the once splendid city of Melinda' that a British Admiralty chart could find.</p></aside>
    </section>

