    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/swahili"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/swahili.jpg" alt="Swahili" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manuscrit_colonial_kiswahili-Africa_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ji-Elle, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Malindi grew out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> civilisation that emerged on the East African coast in the fifth to tenth centuries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantu-peoples">Bantu-speaking</a> farmers moved into the area about Malindi Bay, at the mouth of the Galana River, where they smelted iron, built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and wattle houses thatched with palm leaves, spoke a local dialect of Swahili and traded regionally and at times over long distances. The revival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean-trade">Indian Ocean trade</a> at the end of the first millennium brought larger settlements and greater social complexity, and from the eleventh century the Swahili of the coast acted as middlemen for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a>, Egyptian, Nubian, Arab, Persian and Indian traders, built walled towns and coral houses, and saw their elites convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and often speak Arabic.</p><p>The Malindi Kingdom appears to have formed around the ninth century. The first city, founded around 850 somewhat to the north of the modern town, was destroyed around 1000; signs of habitation are sparse for the next two centuries, and recovery and prosperity came in the 1200s. The first written reference is probably that of the Kurdish geographer and historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-fida">Abu al-Fida</a> (1273–1331), who placed Malindi south of the mouth of a river rising in a mountain hundreds of miles away, perhaps Mount Kenya, where the Galana rises; the town has thus existed as a Swahili settlement since at least the thirteenth century. Its people converted to Islam largely between the thirteenth and fourteenth centuries.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ivory-trade.jpg" alt="Ivory trade" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">Ivory trade</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fang_traders_with_ivory.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown via Fleming H. Revell Company, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Malindi was a port city whose prosperity rested on the export of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and rhinoceros horn and of farm produce, coconuts, oranges, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>. In 1498 it was a prosperous town of between 5,000 and 10,000 people, most of them Muslim. Its society was that of the medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> towns: the ruling class, the wazee, were the heads of the wealthiest patrician families, who spoke both Swahili and Arabic, built in coral stone and claimed descent from the East. Below them lived the merchants, craftsmen, sailors and labourers of a trading port, and after 1505 the town grew as other Swahili, Arab, Persian and Indian merchants, craftsmen, sailors and labourers flocked to the newly powerful city. The Juma Mosque and other buildings of Swahili architecture survive from the town.</p><p>Trade reached far. The fleet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-he">Zheng He</a> called in 1414, and Malindi's ruler sent a personal envoy with a giraffe as a present to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. From 1499 the Portuguese kept a trading post in the town as a rest stop on the way to and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and from 1502 to 1593 a factory, and the town was the centre of Portuguese activity in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. Before the Portuguese came Malindi was a regional power but lagged well behind the two greatest states of the coast, Mombasa and Kilwa; it was the decline of those two under Portuguese attack that let Malindi flourish. A Portuguese chapel with a graveyard stood before 1542, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-xavier">Francis Xavier</a> visited the town.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama.jpg" alt="Vasco da Gama" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ignoto_portoghese,_ritratto_di_un_cavaliere_dell%27ordine_di_cristo,_1525-50_ca._02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sailko, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Malindi's strength lay in alliance rather than arms. The town was rivalled only by Mombasa for dominance in its part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>, and the hostility between them shaped its policy: when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a> arrived in 1498 after a hostile reception in Mombasa, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Malindi received him warmly, signed a trade agreement and supplied a guide for the voyage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The wazee eagerly welcomed the trading post the Portuguese established in 1499, seeking to use Portuguese military might to establish themselves over their rivals in Mombasa, and in 1500 King Dom Manuel I offered the town <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> status.</p><p>As the chief East African ally of Portugal along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> coast, Malindi supported the Portuguese conquest of the rival powers Kilwa and Mombasa in 1505. The fall of those two cities, the greatest states of the coast, raised Malindi in their place, and the Portuguese factory of 1502 made the town the centre of their activity in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> for ninety years. The last act of its power was also won by others' arms: in 1592 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segeju">Segeju</a> occupied Mombasa and in the end surrendered it to the Sheikh of Malindi, who moved his court there and ruled from 1593 to 1630. He invited his Portuguese allies to build a garrison in Mombasa, and they dominated the city. Malindi's rulers never defeated Mombasa in the field; they outlasted it by choosing the side of the newcomers who did.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Like the other medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> towns, Malindi was ruled by its patricians. The ruling class, the wazee, was made up of the heads of the wealthiest families, and, as among other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantu-peoples">Bantu-speaking</a> peoples, these clan leaders elected a mwenye mui, a chief who spoke on behalf of the patricians. The Portuguese mistakenly titled these men 'kings', misunderstanding the nature of Swahili political organisation, and their accounts speak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Malindi who received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a> and of the Sheikh of Malindi who later ruled Mombasa. The wazee spoke both Swahili and Arabic and claimed mythological origins in the East, most often <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, claims that set them apart from the townspeople and the farmers of the hinterland.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> was the religion of government as of the town: the majority had converted between the thirteenth and fourteenth centuries, and by 1498 most of the people were Muslim. Diplomacy was the elders' chief instrument. In 1414 the ruler sent his own envoy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-he">Zheng He</a>'s fleet; in 1498 the authorities signed a trade agreement with Vasco da Gama; from 1500 the town accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> status under the King of Portugal, and a Portuguese factory operated inside it from 1502 to 1593. In 1593 the seat of rule itself moved: the Sheikh of Malindi, given Mombasa by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segeju">Segeju</a>, transferred his court there and governed from Mombasa until 1630, with a Portuguese garrison beside him. The old town was left to decline.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/francis-xavier"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francis-xavier.jpg" alt="Francis Xavier" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/francis-xavier">Francis Xavier</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Franciscus_de_Xabier.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Malindi's choice in 1498 opened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> coast to Portugal. By receiving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a> when Mombasa had turned him away, signing a trade agreement and lending him a guide for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, the town gave the Portuguese their first East African ally, a rest stop on the India run from 1499 and a factory from 1502. Its support for the conquest of Kilwa and Mombasa in 1505 helped bring down the two greatest states of the coast and began the Portuguese colonisation of the region, which the historians of the Swahili towns count as the start of their civilisation's decline. For ninety years Malindi was the centre of Portuguese activity in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and its merchants, craftsmen and sailors prospered on the ruin of their rivals.</p><p>The town also stood at the far end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s maritime reach: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-he">Zheng He</a>'s visit of 1414 and the giraffe sent back to China record a direct exchange between the Swahili coast and the Ming court, and Chinese coins of the fifteenth century lie in the Mambrui dunes. What Malindi built in stone has lasted: the Vasco da Gama Pillar, the Portuguese Chapel built before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-xavier">Francis Xavier</a>'s visit of 1542, the House of Columns and the Juma Mosque are heritage sites of the modern town, which remains a largely Muslim community. The transfer of the Sheikh's court to Mombasa in 1593 made that city, with its Portuguese garrison, the pivot of the coast for the centuries that followed.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Malindi's rise undid it. When the Portuguese moved their main base to Mombasa in 1593, the Sheikh of Malindi, to whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segeju">Segeju</a> had surrendered that city, moved his court there and ruled Mombasa from 1593 to 1630 under the protection of a Portuguese garrison. The factory that had stood in Malindi since 1502 closed the same year. Deprived of its ruler, its Portuguese trade and its place as the centre of their activity in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, the old town declined gradually after the Portuguese left, until by the end of the seventeenth century it had almost disappeared.</p><p>A British Admiralty chart of 'Melinda' in 1823 declared that there were no vestiges of the once splendid city apart from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a>'s pillar, and in 1845 the missionary Ludwig Krapf found the town overgrown by vegetation and uninhabited. The buildings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> architecture that survive, among them the Juma Mosque and the Portuguese chapel, date from before this abandonment. Malindi was refounded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Majid of Zanzibar in 1861 and until the end of the nineteenth century served as a centre of the slave trade; a plan of 1873 shows the area then occupied. In 1890 the town came under British administration, which abolished the slave trade and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, and the loss of enslaved labour brought a sharp fall in agricultural production. Officially made a town in 1903, Malindi had about 1,148 people ten years later and grew in the twentieth century into a resort.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Malindi was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> trading town on the Kenyan coast, ruled by its patrician elders, that received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-he">Zheng He</a> in 1414 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a> in 1498. As Portugal's chief East African ally it rose on the fall of Kilwa and Mombasa in 1505, held a Portuguese factory for ninety years, and faded when its Sheikh moved his court to Mombasa in 1593.</em></p>

